  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v7.0.1 — permission requests work on Android 13 and older.\n\nFIXED\n• Android 13 and older (#65): RequestPermissions opened no permission screen while nothing was granted yet, and a by-interval aggregate returned no buckets.\n• Android (#66): grouped energy aggregates were 1000 times too small (350 kcal read as 0.35); on Android 13 and older energy records and weight were too.\n\nPREVIOUS RELEASE — v7.0.0: permission requests report every permission, and aggregated reads can skip the permission screen.\n\nBREAKING CHANGES\n• Aggregated reads (#63): GetAggregatedHealthData, ByInterval and ByCalendarPeriod take shouldCheckPermissions before the CancellationToken; pass a token by name.\n• DeniedPermissions (#63): RequestPermissionResult lists HealthPermissionDto instead of platform permission strings.\n• HealthPermissionStatus (#63): iOS reads are Undisclosed and unreadable statuses are Unavailable; NotDetermined now means an unasked iOS write only.\n• Android (#63): refused full-history or background access no longer fails RequestPermissions; see FullHistoryReadStatus and BackgroundReadStatus.\n• RequestPermissions (#63): an exception is reported as RequestPermissionError.UnexpectedException.\n• Cancellation (#63): a cancelled RequestPermissions returns RequestPermissionError.Cancelled instead of throwing; a screen already showing stays until answered.\n• Reads and writes (#63): one cancelled while waiting for its permission request reports Cancelled; writes and updates report PermissionDenied only for a refusal.\n• Reads (#63): a read or aggregate on a device without the health platform reports ReadHealthDataError.SdkUnavailable, not only an exception.\n• PermissionType (#63): Read is 1 and Write 2, so the flags combine; stored numbers change, and a write-only permission no longer asks for read access too.\n• HealthPermissionDto (#63): a PermissionType with neither Read nor Write set throws instead of requesting nothing.\n• Statuses (#63): every platform reports one status per read and per write, also when Unavailable.\n• RequestPermissionResult (#63): its properties are init-only; DeniedPermissions was settable.\n• Windows and Mac Catalyst (#63): RequestPermissions returns SdkUnavailable instead of success.\n\nNEW\n• shouldCheckPermissions (#63): false on aggregated reads fails a read with a missing permission instead of opening the permission screen, on iOS too.\n• ReadHealthDataError.PermissionDenied (#63): Android reads report a missing read permission as a typed error.\n• PermissionStatuses (#63): RequestPermissionResult reports every requested permission, so no GetPermissionStatuses call is needed after a request.\n• IsPermissionScreenShown (#63): tells whether the platform showed its permission screen at all.\n• OpenPermissionSettings (#63): opens Health Connect's settings, where the user manages the app's permissions (Android).\n\nFIXED\n• Android (#63): parallel permission requests run one after another instead of leaving invisible activities that swallow every tap.\n• Android (#63): a permission request whose activity is recreated under the permission screen waits for the user, then reads the grants back.\n• Android (#63): full-history access is requested only where Health Connect supports it.\n• Android (#63): data types and the extra access are requested apart, so an extra refused twice no longer hides the data-type screen.\n• GetPermissionStatuses (#63): never throws; without a current activity or Health Connect it reports Unavailable.\n• Android workouts (#63): reads and writes no longer need a current activity.\n\nPREVIOUS RELEASE — v6.0.0: the health data types move to Kebechet.Types.Health, a package without MAUI, under its own namespaces.\n\nBREAKING CHANGES\n• Namespaces (#62): DTOs, enums, results and their helpers are now in Kebechet.Types.Health.* — replace using Maui.Health.Models with using Kebechet.Types.Health.Models (same for .Enums, .Extensions, .Constants).\n\nNEW\n• Kebechet.Types.Health (#62): the DTOs, enums and results as a plain .NET package, for code shared with a watch app or a backend.\n• HealthDataSdk.GoogleHealthServices (#62): marks workouts recorded on a Wear OS watch with Health Services.\n\nCHANGED\n• WorkoutSession (#62): its constructor and the DTO-to-permission helpers are public, so other health libraries can use them.",
